  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Corydaldine may be associated with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through a series of target relationships. Specifically, Corydaldine is linked to FaDu, which is a target of multiple compounds, including (2S)-3-(3,4-dihydroxyphenyl)-2-[(E)-3-[4-hydroxy-3-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxyphenyl]prop-2-enoyl]oxypropanoic acid, 2,5-Dimethylbenzaldehyde, and 3,3'-Triiodothyronine. All of these compounds are known to interact with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. The scores for these relationships are consistent, indicating a potential mechanism by which Corydaldine may influence the complex. The evidence suggests that Corydaldine could act as a modulator or inhibitor of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, possibly through the activation or suppression of its downstream targets. The relationship is supported by multiple independent pathways, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
